 \begin{abstract}
-Raspberry Pi systems are widely used for edge AI, yet the Raspberry Pi~5 inference stack still treats its Arm CPU and VideoCore~VII QPUs as separate targets: using both processors for inference requires coordinating native kernels, tensor representations, and model-runtime scheduling. We build a native CPU--QPU runtime to investigate when this execution path can reduce inference latency. The implementation combines FP32 and W8A8 kernels with persistent programs and weights, mapped tensor storage, explicit dependencies, and concurrent operator partitions. We evaluate operators and model execution against optimized CPU implementations, including OpenBLAS and \texttt{llama.cpp} CPU\_REPACK, across vision, language, and vision--language--action workloads. Exploratory FP32 measurements identify selected projection improvements of 1.12--1.36$\times$ and stage improvements of up to 2.30$\times$. We introduce channel-partitioned FFN execution that reuses production activation representations and retains QPU intermediates, reducing full-prompt latency by 3.31\% and cached-request latency by 3.65\% against CPU\_REPACK. Evaluations on MMLU, ImageNet, COCO, and SmolVLA replay show comparable task accuracy and action outputs for the tested native-format LLM and FP32 paths. The measurements identify how representation, preparation, and execution boundaries determine whether native QPU work improves inference, providing practical guidance for code generation and heterogeneous runtime optimization on Raspberry Pi~5.
